{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 defines reliability as variance/worst-case minimization, but the problem requires comparing reliability 'on average' for 'tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes.' Worst-case minimization is a different objective and need not coincide with higher mean reliability; a system can have lower variance yet lower average correctness, so SRC_001 does not establish the comparative reliability the problem asks for.","counterexample":[{"a":"Worst-case system accuracy is 0.5 with zero variance","b":"Expert mean accuracy is 0.7 with occasional 0.2 off-days","c":"Panel+checklist is lower variance but not more reliable on average"}],"target":"2eb76d96312cb0ea1404f833bbd1ee47389af31061394aab0a72860ea3b34d4f"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 attacks the panel by asserting checklists impose linear decomposition, but it never gives a causal mechanism for why a trained non-expert panel using a checklist is worse at interdependent tasks than a single expert. The claim hinges on the expert's 'subconscious integration of non-linear patterns,' but no evidence within a single evaluation is specified to show that this intuition produces more reliable verifiable outcomes rather than merely a confident judgment that could be biased. Per the problem's standard, evidence must distinguish genuine reliability gain from compliance tracking the right answer; SRC_02 provides no such distinguishing evidence for the expert's intuition.","counterexample":[{"a":"Expert intuitively flags a case as 'wrong' but is incorrect","b":"Panel checks every box and is correct","c":"No single-evaluation evidence distinguishes biased intuition from genuine pattern recognition"}],"target":"3beab44ff5f1406864aee497040c19d810a43ddbee959200e8675fe8cad84626"},{"attack":false,"case":"","target":"5e4034eaad04abc10d8f3c956708fe9ccbe417f452a6ad6ea5746e745f010637"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_004 asserts that checklists impose linear decomposition and experts handle non-linear dependencies via compressed pattern recognition, but it provides no single-evaluation evidence rule to distinguish whether the expert's intuition is genuinely synthesizing variables or merely producing a confident answer that happens to be right. The problem explicitly asks what evidence within one evaluation separates a genuine reliability gain from compliance that merely tracks the right answer; SRC_04 never addresses that requirement for either side, and its countercondition about 'formally mapped into a hierarchical checklist' does not specify an observable discriminator within a single evaluation.","counterexample":[{"a":"Expert's synthesis judgment is correct by chance on one evaluation","b":"Panel checklist is also correct","c":"No within-evaluation evidence specified to attribute expert success to genuine synthesis rather than coincidence"}],"target":"c7a3b1df837d7c3d49f107add1a096c7ae9aecfe63939143c07567e95d40e45c"}]}